{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":54077442,"ns":0,"title":"Berkeley school of political theory","revisions":[{"revid":1317820560,"parentid":1314276873,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2025-10-20T06:02:06Z","size":3181,"sha1":"d56a0fb790cd0b79c95836701cd88ea4ec06ad8e","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"The '''Berkeley school of political theory''' is a [[school of thought]] in [[political theory]] associated originally with the work of faculty at the [[University of California, Berkeley]], some of whom formulated and popularized its ideas. The school of thought was founded by [[Sheldon Wolin]], [[Hanna Pitkin]],<ref>\"In light of Pitkin's long association with Berkeley, it is no surprise that academic lore frequently connects her work to a 'Berkeley School' of political theory, said to have flourished in the 1960s and '70s.\", Mathiowetz, 2016, p.5 of the PDF.</ref>  [[Michael Rogin]], [[John Schaar]], and Norman Jacobson, among others, and has been carried on by theorists including [[Wilson Carey McWilliams]], [[Mary G. Dietz]], [[Linda M.G. Zerilli]] and [[J. Peter Euben]].<ref name=\"grimes-obit\">William Grimes, [https://www.nytimes.com/2015/10/29/us/politics/sheldon-s-wolin-theorist-who-shifted-political-science-back-to-politics-dies-at-93.html \"Sheldon S. Wolin, Theorist Who Shifted Political Science Back to Politics, Dies at 93\"] (obituary), ''[[The New York Times]]'', Oct. 28, 2015.</ref><ref name=\"mathiowetz-2017\">Mathiowetz 2017.</ref>\n\nThe Berkeley school has been characterized as returning political theory back in the direction of politics and political action, and away from efforts to center \"scientific\" models such as economics, psychology, sociology, and the natural sciences, specifically [[behavioralism]] and [[evolutionary psychology]].<ref name=\"mathiowetz-2016\">Mathiowetz, 2016.</ref><ref name=\"grimes-obit\" />\n\nHanna Pitkin herself disputed the existence of the Berkeley school as a school of thought, citing substantial differences between theoretical approaches.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Pitkin |first=Hanna |last2=Rosenblum |first2=Nancy |date=2015-05-11 |title=A Conversation with Hanna Pitkin |url=https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev-polisci-092514-012354 |journal=Annual Review of Political Science |language=en |volume=18 |issue=  |pages=1–10 |doi=10.1146/annurev-polisci-092514-012354|url-access=subscription }}</ref> In particular, Pitkin took issue with the idea that Sheldon Wolin's views on political theory as a discipline and politics as a practice were representative of the perspectives of the political theorists often grouped in the Berkeley school.\n\n==References==\n{{Reflist}}\n\n== Further reading ==\n* D. P. Mathiowetz, \"The Berkeley School of Political Theory as Moment and as Tradition\", ''PS: Political Science and Politics'', June 1, 2017, available at https://escholarship.org/uc/item/3n9196dc.\n* James P. Young, ''Reconsidering American Liberalism: The Troubled Odyssey of the Liberal Idea'' (Boulder: Westview Press, 1996), 294–306.\n* Jason Reiner, \"The Berkeley School of Political Theory: A Retrospective on an American Radicalism,\" ''Association for Political Theory 2013 Annual Meeting''\n* D. P. Mathiowetz, 2016, \"Hanna Fenichel Pitkin and the Dilemmas of Political Thinking\", in ''Hanna Fenichel Pitkin: Politics, Judgement, Action'', available at https://escholarship.org/uc/item/1x84g1jx .\n\n[[Category:Political theories]]\n[[Category:Schools of thought]]\n\n\n{{polisci-stub}}"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-10-06T11:32:19Z","lastrevid":1317820560,"length":3181}]}}